===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make a ruffle in; to curl or [[flute]], ''as'' an edge of fabric.
#: {{ux|en|'''Ruffle''' the end of the cuff.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[disturb]]; especially, to cause to [[flutter]].
#: {{ux|en|The wind '''ruffled''' the papers.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her sudden volley of insults '''ruffled''' his composure.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|Palamon and Arcite|passage=She {{...}}smoothed the '''ruffled''' seas.}}
#* {{RQ:Isaac Taylor Saturday Evening|passage=the fantastic revelries {{...}} that so often '''ruffled''' the placid bosom of the Nile}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Idylls|pages=227–228|pageref=227|passage=But, ever after, the small violence done / Rankled in him and '''ruffled''' all his heart, / As the sharp wind that '''ruffles''' all day long / A little bitter pool about a stone / On the bare coast.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1860|author={{w|Sir William Hamilton, 9th Baronet|Sir William Hamilton}}|title=Sir William Hamilton, 9th Baronet
|passage=These '''ruffle''' the tranquillity of the mind.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1972|author=w:Roald Dahl|title=w:Charlie and the Great Glass Elevator|page=114|publisher=Knopf
|passage="Ladies and gentlemen," said Mr. Wonka, coming up close and raising both hands for silence. "''Please'', I beg you, do not '''ruffle''' yourselves! There's nothing to worry about."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Rees|title=Finn Russell masterminds historic Scotland victory over England|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180422092213/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2018/feb/24/scotland-england-six-nations-match-report|archivedate=22 April 2018|newspaper=w:The Guardian|location=London|date=24 February 2018|passage=[[w:Finn Russell|[Finn] Russell]] created his side’s three first-half tries, '''ruffling''' a defence known for its composure, and dictated the match from the off.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To grow rough, boisterous, or turbulent.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|act=II|scene=iv|passage=The night comes on, and the bleak winds / Do sorely '''ruffle'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become disordered; to play loosely; to flutter.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|book=3|passage=On his right shoulder his thick mane ', / '''Ruffles''' at speed, and dances in the wind.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be rough; to jar; to be in contention; hence, to put on airs; to swagger.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Henry 7|passage=They would '''ruffle''' with jurors.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Peveril of the Peak|passage=gallants who '''ruffled''' in silk and embroidery}}
# To make into a ruff; to draw or contract into puckers, plaits, or folds; to wrinkle.
# To erect in a ruff, as feathers.
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=II|poem=Morte d'Arthur|page=16|passage=[T]he barge with oar and sail / Moved from the brink, like some full-breasted swan / That, fluting a wild carol ere her death, / '''Ruffles''' her pure cold plume, and takes the flood / With swarthy webs.}}
# {{lb|en|military}} To beat with the [[ruff]] or ruffle, as a drum.
# To throw together in a disorderly manner.
#* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Odysseys|book=7|page=165|lines=395–397|passage=Within a thicket I reposed; when round / I '''ruffled''' up fall'n leaves in heap; and found, / Let fall from heaven, a sleep interminate.}}

